I think that score makes primarily sense for beginners to have some
indication how they proceed and evolve. At the latest if you ascend
on a regular basis the score becomes uninteresting (but some people
nontheless try for reasons of behavioural game analysis to overflow
internal counters).

To conclude, I wouldn't use that rude word above, since I see some
use of the score. But at any contest where the best people compete
the score is an inappropriate measure. And uninteresting anyway for
people who like the game more than watching the numbers in a list.

While the high score list at NAO is populated by few score crunchers
NAO cares about other measures. The ones I appreciate most are the
top-all-class-players (while score based still value the diversity
of played classes) and the deepest dungeon reached (which includes
also an ascended-games-per-all-games-ratio measure).

WRT the contest, I agree in principle with Rast's score suggestion
(differences only in some specific values; especially the speed
ascension scale, which seems to be biased by Rast's proficiency,
should be stretched _a bit_ ;-).

